# Radios

Use radios when exactly one answer can be selected from a short list.

These are civica reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## Choose one answer, not a device command

The [forms decision guide](controls.html#choose-the-control-by-its-meaning) explains which primitive fits the intent. This page owns one-answer selection and native radio-group interaction. Keep live device on/off controls and action requests under their native capability and state contract.

## When to use it

Use them for a new unit choice, a display mode, or a small set of mutually exclusive options. Keep the choices visible when comparing them is useful.

## When not to use it

Do not use radios for selecting several rooms or for an immediate physical on/off control. A switch and a question with two answers have different interaction expectations.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Show a saved setting when editing an existing preference. For a new unanswered question, avoid silently choosing an answer. Include a genuine “Use system setting” or “Not sure” option when it is valid, rather than forcing a false binary answer.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use one fieldset and legend, one shared name, distinct values and a label for each control. Arrow keys move through a native radio group. Keep follow-up questions simple and do not hide essential explanation behind an option.

